economics.solution_overview:
The extract reports revenue, cost of sales, and opening/closing balances for total assets, inventory and trade receivables (EUR thousands). Average stock for a turnover ratio is (opening + closing) ÷ 2. Inventory turnover uses cost of sales over average inventory; asset turnover uses revenue over average total assets; receivables turnover uses revenue over average trade receivables.

$$
\text{Inventory turnover} = \frac{\text{cost of sales}}{\text{average inventory}}
$$

$$
\text{Asset turnover} = \frac{\text{revenue}}{\text{average total assets}}
$$

$$
\text{Receivables turnover} = \frac{\text{revenue}}{\text{average trade receivables}}
$$

Current assets are inventory + trade receivables + cash (119 + 60 + 97 = 276); current liabilities are trade payables + bank overdraft (74 + 58 = 132). Non-current assets total 307 + 153 + 58 + 66 = 584; total assets are 860. After inventory is stripped out, the quick (acid-test) cover is (CA − inventory) ÷ CL.

$$
\text{Current ratio} = \frac{\text{CA}}{\text{CL}}
$$

$$
\text{Quick ratio} = \frac{\text{CA} - \text{inventory}}{\text{CL}}
$$

$$
\text{Working capital} = \text{CA} - \text{CL}
$$

